    "knowledge_point": "Simplifying a Variable Expression Containing a Quotient of Factorials",
    "problem": "Given that $n\\ge -1$ is an integer, simplify $\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)!}.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n(n+2)!=(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)!\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel the $(n+1)!$ that occurs in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)!} & =\\frac{(n+1)!}{(n+2)\\cdot (n+1)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(n+1)!}}{(n+2)\\cdot \\cancel{(n+1)!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
